    {
      "part": "problem",
      "original_record": "before",
      "value": "Let $I=Z$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i-1,\\,i\\},$ then ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}=$",
      "original_html_sha256": "57038fcead0473a57047c8de5fbdc577152a7ddb2b0071340fa2eac382bf8a0d",
      "extractor_sha256": "1591183da328a4140792793d2cc113fd535ab5f25bc7f284ceb1b6f251efad18",
      "source_file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071758/repairs/source-provenance-v2/structured-source/source/57038fcead0473a57047c8de5fbdc577152a7ddb2b0071340fa2eac382bf8a0d.html",
      "extraction_errors": []
    },
    {
      "part": "worked_solution",
      "original_record": "history",
      "value": "The intersection of a family of indexed sets $\\{{A}_{i}\\}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for all }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=-1,$ we have ${X}_{-1}=\\{-2,-1\\}$\n\n- if $i=0,$ we have ${X}_{0}=\\{-1,0\\}$\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have ${X}_{1}=\\{0,1\\}$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that there is no element that belongs to all the sets. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}=∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "original_html_sha256": "f2b77a2e11c47cf152932a6cb24ba5fe45aa20d8632080f5286e53dd5b7a8eb9",
      "extractor_sha256": "1591183da328a4140792793d2cc113fd535ab5f25bc7f284ceb1b6f251efad18",
